WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.260
Good morning John, hey, look!

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:06.910
It's Portland, Oregon, outside that window,
also in this hotel room, I guess?

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:08.160
I mean, what is space?

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:14.550
I'm up in the air, fourteen stories, and I'm
inside of a hotel room that is owned by, I

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.200
assume, a hotel chain.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.940
But am I even in any of those places?

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:21.490
Am I not just in the space between my neurons?

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:22.920
Is that Portland?

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:28.710
Is that area between my neurons Portland just
because I am within the boundaries of the

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.740
city of Portland, Oregon?

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:31.740
Ugh.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:37.820
As you can see just by the conversation I
just had with you I've been hanging out with

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:41.170
the creators of "Welcome to Night Vale", Joseph
Fink and Jeffrey Cranor.

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:45.379
The book just came out, debuted number four
on the New York Times best-seller list, number

00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:48.139
four because Nicholas Sparks, J.K.

00:00:48.139 --> 00:00:50.750
Rowling, and John Grisham came out with new
books this week.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.149
Bad week to try and get to number one.

00:00:53.149 --> 00:00:56.649
I was with Jeffrey and Joseph when they got
the news that they were number four on the

00:00:56.649 --> 00:01:00.629
New York Times best-seller list, and that
was a great experience for me not only because

00:01:00.629 --> 00:01:05.030
the Chinese crepes from the Portland food
cart we were eating were amazing, but also

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:07.320
because it was good to see that happen to
a person.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.850
I'm feeling thankful right now.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:13.390
I'm feeling thankful for like three or four
things, uh, just great comments on the last

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:14.670
couple videos that we've made.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.820
Remarkable how well-received my Benghazi video
was.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:22.270
I received lots of hate, just to be clear,
but, like, the like-to-dislike-ratio was pretty

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:27.799
amazing, which makes me feel like there's
a lot of interest in this kind of content.

00:01:27.799 --> 00:01:29.520
I mean, it doesn't go super viral.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:37.049
Let's be honest: if my video was about getting
riled up and angry about one or the other

00:01:37.049 --> 00:01:42.829
side, how wrong they were, how evil they were,
how they were trying to destroy America - and

00:01:42.829 --> 00:01:45.640
that's literally the rhetoric that people
use in these conversations - it would have

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:46.640
gotten more views.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.840
It would have made people want to stand up
on the mountaintops and shout to their people

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.030
and it would have gotten more than 300,000
views.

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:55.030
But!

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:59.420
300,000 views, it's a tremendous amount it's
the kind of influence that high-school or

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:02.859
college Hank could only have ever dreamed
of having!

00:02:02.859 --> 00:02:04.240
So, what is the goal?

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.240
What is the important thing here?

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.520
That we use the platform we have to get more
views?

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.890
Grow a bigger platform, and then we use that
platform to grow a bigger platform and then

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:18.470
that bigger platform we can then use to grow
a bigger platform?

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:19.470
Is that the only goal?

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:25.320
At a certain point you have to say that, like,
doing things that you know, as a person, are

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:31.100
bad for individual people and culture as a
whole are not worth doing just to get a bigger

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:37.050
audience, and you use the platform you have,
and God am I thankful to have this platform,

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:39.330
to do something you think is good.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:46.840
Making content that removes the charge, that
removes the fear, of the other fifty percent

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:52.900
of Americans from the political discourse,
that would be extremely valuable.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:58.090
I also wanna say thanks to all the people
who left really positive, constructive, fascinating

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:00.350
comments on John's YouTube Red video.

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:05.580
I just installed YouTube Red, I love, I'm
just digging not having advertisements.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:12.040
There's the thirty day free trial, signed
up for it, and it's - it's just a dream, honestly.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:13.790
It's just me and my subscriptions.

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:19.110
It's nice, I get what all those people who
have ad-blocker installed, I understand why

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:20.110
you do it.

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:24.690
I've never done it because it's literally
how I pay my employees so I'd feel a little

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:27.200
bad about that, but I totally understand.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.500
At the moment, John, I'm just inspired to
do things that I think are cool, are interesting

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:35.510
that I think people would like.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:37.950
That's really, I guess that's all we've ever
wanted to do.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:43.510
And we get to do it.

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:44.510
We're so lucky.

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:45.690
Thanks, everybody.

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.090
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:50.950
I ate a whole lot of pizza before I made this
video.

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:53.260
I do not feel great.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:55.390
I feel less than great.

